    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my diesel mech exam in munich and i'm a bit stuck on turbochargers. can u explain how the exhaust gas actually turns the turbine and how that leads to more air getting into the engine? specifically how the pressure increases in the manifold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exhaust gases, which are hot and pressurized, are directed into the turbocharger's turbine wheel. As these gases expand and rush over the turbine blades, they impart kinetic energy, causing the turbine to spin at very high speeds. This spinning turbine is connected by a shaft to the compressor wheel, forcing ambient air into the intake side. This forced induction compresses the air, significantly increasing its pressure before it enters the engine's intake manifold.",
      "source": "target",
